## Technology Guidelines

### Angular Best Practices

**MANDATORY: Always reference docs/cursor/angularStandards.md before any Angular development work.**

This document is the canonical source for all Angular standards and must be consulted for:

- Creating new Angular components, services, or modules
- Refactoring existing Angular code
- Conducting code reviews
- Making architectural decisions
- Troubleshooting SonarQube violations

Each Angular file must be separated as follows:

- .ts → component logic
- .scss → all styling for component
- .html → markup for page

**Requirements:**

- All Angular components must be created as standalone
- Components: Standalone components only (.ts/.scss/.html separation)
- Services: Injectable with providedIn: 'root' for singletons
- State Management: Use signals for reactive state when appropriate
- Forms: Prefer reactive forms over template-driven
- Testing: Include unit tests for complex business logic
- Follow SonarQube "Sonar Way" quality profile standards as defined in angularStandards.md
- Use modern Angular patterns and avoid deprecated approaches as specified in the standards document

### Azure DevOps Work Item Comment Formatting Standards

When adding comments to Azure DevOps work items, always use proper HTML formatting instead of markdown to ensure professional appearance and proper rendering. Follow these formatting standards:

#### Required HTML Tags:
- Use `<h2>` and `<h3>` for headers (not ## or ###)
- Use `<p>` for paragraphs (not plain text)
- Use `<ul>` and `<ol>` with `<li>` for lists (not - or 1.)
- Use `<pre><code>` for code blocks (not ```)
- Use `<strong>` for emphasis (not **text**)

#### Structure Requirements:
- Always structure comments with clear sections and proper hierarchy
- Use proper indentation and spacing
- Include complete technical details, root cause analysis, and testing requirements
- Ensure professional presentation with clean formatting

This ensures all Azure DevOps work item comments display professionally and are easy to read.

### SQL File Management - CRITICAL REQUIREMENTS

#### Prerequisites (MANDATORY for all SQL work)

Before working on any SQL file (.sql, .prc, .fnc, .tbl), you MUST:

1. **Read sqlStandards.md** - Always review and follow the established SQL coding standards
2. **Request Task Information** - Prompt user for required information

#### Required Information Prompt

When starting SQL work, use this template:

```
Before I begin working (this means changing, you can read files without asking) on SQL files, I need the following information:
- **Task ID**: [What is the current task/ticket ID?]
- **Date**: [Today's date in DD-MMM-YYYY format]
```

#### Revision History Standards

- Always update revision history with format: "<DD-MMM-YYYY> - <[Name of User]> - <[Task ID]> - [Change Summary]"
- Place revision history at the top of each modified file
- Include meaningful change descriptions, not just "updated file"
- Reference related files if changes span multiple procedures/functions

#### Compliance Requirements

- Follow all standards defined in sqlStandards.md
- Use consistent naming conventions (pr/fn/tbl prefixes as established)
- Include rollback scripts for schema changes
- Test procedures with sample data before finalizing
- Comment complex logic and business rules
- Ensure proper error handling in stored procedures

#### SQL Work Initialization

- When any task involves SQL files, IMMEDIATELY prompt for Task ID and date
- Do not proceed with SQL modifications until this information is provided
- Reference sqlStandards.md before making any database changes
- Confirm understanding of the change requirements before implementation

